<body>

<header>
<div class="container">
<h1>Global Cost of Living Crisis Index 2026</h1>
<p class="subtitle">A Comparative Analysis of Economic Stress Across 80 Major Cities and 16 Regions Worldwide</p>
<p class="meta">Prepared for policy analysts and economic researchers &bull; Data year: 2026 &bull; Baseline: New York, USA (index = 100)</p>
</div>
</header>

<div class="container">

<nav class="toc">
<h2>Table of Contents</h2>
<ol>
<li><a href="#executive-overview">Executive Overview &amp; Key Performance Indicators</a></li>
<li><a href="#regional-comparison">Regional Cost-of-Living Comparison</a></li>
<li><a href="#affordability-analysis">Affordability and Rent Burden Analysis</a></li>
<li><a href="#crisis-tier-profiling">Crisis Tier Profiling</a></li>
<li><a href="#inflation-oil-exposure">Inflation and Oil Shock Exposure</a></li>
<li><a href="#key-findings-recommendations">Key Findings and Policy Implications</a></li>
<li><a href="#appendix">Appendix: Full City Dataset</a></li>
</ol>
</nav>

<!-- ============ EXECUTIVE OVERVIEW ============ -->
<section id="executive-overview">
<h2>1. Executive Overview</h2>

<p>This report examines cost-of-living pressures across 80 major cities worldwide in 2026, spanning 16 geographic regions. The analysis reveals a deeply fragmented global economic landscape: while affluent cities in Western Europe and North America face high absolute costs offset by strong purchasing power, cities in South Asia, Sub-Saharan Africa, and conflict-affected regions confront severe affordability crises driven by low incomes, hyperinflation, and geopolitical instability.</p>

<p>All 80 cities in the dataset have rent-to-salary ratios exceeding 50%, indicating that housing affordability is a universal pressure point — but the drivers and severity vary enormously.</p>

<h3>Key Performance Indicators</h3>

<div class="kpi-grid">
<div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-label">Median Cost of Living Index</div>
    <div class="kpi-value">{median_col}</div>
    <div class="kpi-desc">Central benchmark vs. New York (100)</div>
</div>
<div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-label">Average Rent-to-Salary Ratio</div>
    <div class="kpi-value">{avg_rts:.1f}%</div>
    <div class="kpi-desc">Mean share of income consumed by city-center rent</div>
</div>
<div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-label">Purchasing Power Gap</div>
    <div class="kpi-value">{ppp_gap:.1f}</div>
    <div class="kpi-desc">Spread between highest ({ppp_max_city['city']}: {ppp_max_city['local_purchasing_power_index']}) and lowest ({ppp_min_city['city']}: {ppp_min_city['local_purchasing_power_index']})</div>
</div>
<div class="kpi-card" style="border-top-color: var(--accent);">
    <div class="kpi-label">High-Crisis City Count</div>
    <div class="kpi-value" style="color: var(--accent);">{high_crisis_count}</div>
    <div class="kpi-desc">Cities in Tier 5 (most severe crisis)</div>
</div>
<div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-label">Average Annual Inflation</div>
    <div class="kpi-value">{avg_inflation:.1f}%</div>
    <div class="kpi-desc">Mean 2025 inflation rate across all 80 cities</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="callout">
<strong>Headline:</strong> The median city costs 65.5% of New York's level, but <strong>14 of 80 cities (17.5%)</strong> fall in the most severe crisis tier — characterized by average inflation of 28.4% and mean monthly salaries of just $455. The purchasing power gap of 148.7 points between Basel, Switzerland and Buenos Aires, Argentina underscores the extreme inequality in global economic conditions.
</div>

<p>The most expensive city, <strong>{most_expensive['city']}</strong> ({most_expensive['country']}), has a cost-of-living index of {most_expensive['cost_of_living_index']} with an average salary of ${most_expensive['avg_monthly_net_salary_usd']:,}. The least expensive, <strong>{least_expensive['city']}</strong> ({least_expensive['country']}), registers just {least_expensive['cost_of_living_index']} but with an average salary of only ${least_expensive['avg_monthly_net_salary_usd']:,}/month.</p>
</section>

<!-- ============ REGIONAL COMPARISON ============ -->
<section id="regional-comparison">
<h2>2. Regional Cost-of-Living Comparison</h2>

<p>The 80 cities span 16 regions with starkly different economic profiles. Western Europe leads on absolute cost of living (avg. index: 88.6) but also delivers the highest purchasing power (141.2). At the other end, South Asia has the lowest costs (28.9) but the weakest purchasing power (52.5) and lowest salaries ($494/month average).</p>

<div class="chart-box">
<canvas id="chartRegional" height="280"></canvas>
</div>

<h3>Regional Averages</h3>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Region</th><th>Cities</th><th>Avg CoL Index</th><th>Avg Rent Index</th><th>Avg PPP Index</th><th>Avg Salary</th><th>Avg Rent/Salary</th><th>Avg Inflation</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
{reg_table_rows}
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h3>Regional Outliers</h3>
<p>Cities that deviate significantly from their regional norms deserve particular attention from policymakers, as they may face distinct structural challenges.</p>

<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>City</th><th>Region</th><th>CoL Index</th><th>Region Avg CoL</th><th>Deviation</th><th>Rent/Salary</th><th>Region Avg R/S</th><th>Deviation</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
{outlier_html}
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="callout-warn callout">
<strong>Notable outliers:</strong> Within North America, <strong>New York</strong> (CoL 100.0) sits 17.8 points above the regional mean of 82.2. In Western Europe, the five Swiss cities collectively pull the regional average well above continental peers like Brussels (73.5) and Milan (73.1). In East Asia, <strong>Hong Kong</strong> (CoL 75.2, PPP 91.6) stands apart from lower-cost peers like Beijing (58.0) and Seoul (59.0).
</div>
</section>

<!-- ============ AFFORDABILITY ANALYSIS ============ -->
<section id="affordability-analysis">
<h2>3. Affordability and Rent Burden Analysis</h2>

<p>Housing cost as a share of income is the single most actionable metric for policymakers. A rent-to-salary ratio above 30% is conventionally considered burdensome; above 50% is severe. <strong>Every city in this dataset exceeds 50%</strong>, indicating a global housing affordability crisis — but the nature of the crisis differs dramatically between high-income and low-income cities.</p>

<h3>Rent Burden Distribution</h3>

<div class="callout-danger callout">
<strong>Critical finding:</strong> All 80 cities have rent-to-salary ratios above 50%. The global mean is <strong>{avg_rts:.1f}%</strong>. The highest burden is in <strong>{highest_rts['city']}</strong> ({highest_rts['rent_to_salary_ratio_pct']}%) and the lowest is in <strong>{lowest_rts['city']}</strong> ({lowest_rts['rent_to_salary_ratio_pct']}%). The range is narrow (20.2 percentage points), but the underlying economics are fundamentally different: high-income cities have high rents offset by strong salaries, while low-income cities have low rents that nonetheless consume a large share of meager incomes.
</div>

<div class="chart-box">
<canvas id="chartRentBurden" height="320"></canvas>
</div>

<h3>Top 20 Cities by Rent Burden</h3>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>City</th><th>Country</th><th>Region</th><th>Rent/Salary</th><th>Avg Salary</th><th>1BR Rent (Center)</th><th>CoL Index</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
{afford_table}
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h3>Salary vs. Rent Burden: A Structural Divide</h3>

<p>The scatter plot below reveals two distinct clusters: (1) high-income cities ($3,000+/month) with rent burdens of 62&ndash;72%, where high absolute rents drive the ratio; and (2) low-income cities (&lt;$1,500/month) with rent burdens of 51&ndash;60%, where even modest rents are unaffordable relative to income.</p>

<div class="chart-box">
<canvas id="chartScatter" height="380"></canvas>
</div>

<p>Notably, the highest rent burdens are concentrated in the wealthiest cities — Swiss cities (Zurich 70.8%, Geneva 70.2%), U.S. tech hubs (Seattle 71.2%, San Francisco 69.2%), and Nordic capitals. This suggests that even strong salaries cannot fully offset extreme housing costs in these markets. However, for lower-income cities, the policy concern is more acute: a 53% rent burden on $280/month (Kathmandu) or $165/month (Cairo) leaves virtually no margin for other essentials.</p>

<h3>15 Cities with Lowest Rent Burden</h3>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>City</th><th>Country</th><th>Region</th><th>Rent/Salary</th><th>Avg Salary</th><th>1BR Rent (Center)</th><th>CoL Index</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
{afford_table_bottom}
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="callout">
<strong>Policy implication:</strong> Cities at both extremes of the income spectrum face acute housing affordability issues, but requiring different interventions. High-income cities need supply-side housing policy (construction, zoning reform). Low-income cities need wage growth and income support alongside housing investment.
</div>
</section>

<!-- ============ CRISIS TIER PROFILING ============ -->
<section id="crisis-tier-profiling">
<h2>4. Crisis Tier Profiling</h2>

<p>The dataset classifies each city into one of five crisis tiers (1 = least severe / affluent; 5 = most severe). These tiers capture qualitatively different economic conditions, not just a cost gradient.</p>

<h3>Tier Summary</h3>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Profile</th><th>Cities</th><th>Avg CoL</th><th>Avg Rent Idx</th><th>Avg PPP</th><th>Avg Salary</th><th>Avg R/S Ratio</th><th>Avg Inflation</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
{tier_table_rows}
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="chart-box">
<canvas id="chartTierBar" height="280"></canvas>
</div>

<h3>Tier Distribution</h3>
<div class="chart-box" style="max-width:400px; margin: 24px auto;">
<canvas id="chartTierDonut" height="300"></canvas>
</div>

<h3>Key Differentiators Between Tiers</h3>

<div class="callout">
<strong>What separates Tier 1 from Tier 5:</strong> Tier 1 cities average $6,055/month salary with 1.4% inflation; Tier 5 cities average $455/month with 28.4% inflation. The purchasing power index drops from 157.1 to 48.0 — a 3.3x gap. Paradoxically, Tier 1 has a <em>higher</em> rent-to-salary ratio (67.4%) than Tier 5 (53.6%), because extreme housing costs in affluent cities slightly outpace even their high salaries.
</div>

<div class="chart-box">
<canvas id="chartTierInflation" height="250"></canvas>
</div>

<h3>Cities by Tier</h3>
{tier_city_lists}
</section>

<!-- ============ INFLATION & OIL EXPOSURE ============ -->
<section id="inflation-oil-exposure">
<h2>5. Inflation and Oil Shock Exposure</h2>

<p>Inflation and oil dependency are key structural vulnerabilities. Cities with high oil shock exposure face an average inflation rate of 20.4% — six times that of low-exposure cities (3.4%). This correlation is driven by a cluster of conflict-affected, oil-dependent, and structurally fragile economies.</p>

<h3>Oil Shock Exposure Groups</h3>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
