<section id="affordability-analysis">
<h2>3. Affordability and Rent Burden Analysis</h2>

<p>Housing cost as a share of income is the single most actionable metric for policymakers. A rent-to-salary ratio above 30% is conventionally considered burdensome; above 50% is severe. <strong>Every city in this dataset exceeds 50%</strong>, indicating a global housing affordability crisis — but the nature of the crisis differs dramatically between high-income and low-income cities.</p>

<h3>Rent Burden Distribution</h3>

<div class="callout-danger callout">
<strong>Critical finding:</strong> All 80 cities have rent-to-salary ratios above 50%. The global mean is <strong>{avg_rts:.1f}%</strong>. The highest burden is in <strong>{highest_rts['city']}</strong> ({highest_rts['rent_to_salary_ratio_pct']}%) and the lowest is in <strong>{lowest_rts['city']}</strong> ({lowest_rts['rent_to_salary_ratio_pct']}%). The range is narrow (20.2 percentage points), but the underlying economics are fundamentally different: high-income cities have high rents offset by strong salaries, while low-income cities have low rents that nonetheless consume a large share of meager incomes.
</div>

<div class="chart-box">
<canvas id="chartRentBurden" height="320"></canvas>
</div>

<h3>Top 20 Cities by Rent Burden</h3>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>City</th><th>Country</th><th>Region</th><th>Rent/Salary</th><th>Avg Salary</th><th>1BR Rent (Center)</th><th>CoL Index</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
{afford_table}
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h3>Salary vs. Rent Burden: A Structural Divide</h3>

<p>The scatter plot below reveals two distinct clusters: (1) high-income cities ($3,000+/month) with rent burdens of 62&ndash;72%, where high absolute rents drive the ratio; and (2) low-income cities (&lt;$1,500/month) with rent burdens of 51&ndash;60%, where even modest rents are unaffordable relative to income.</p>

<div class="chart-box">
<canvas id="chartScatter" height="380"></canvas>
</div>

<p>Notably, the highest rent burdens are concentrated in the wealthiest cities — Swiss cities (Zurich 70.8%, Geneva 70.2%), U.S. tech hubs (Seattle 71.2%, San Francisco 69.2%), and Nordic capitals. This suggests that even strong salaries cannot fully offset extreme housing costs in these markets. However, for lower-income cities, the policy concern is more acute: a 53% rent burden on $280/month (Kathmandu) or $165/month (Cairo) leaves virtually no margin for other essentials.</p>

<h3>15 Cities with Lowest Rent Burden</h3>
